<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aistulf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/756">756</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aistulf was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> from 749 to 756, previously duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a>. In 751 he captured Ravenna, ending the Byzantine Exarchate, and then threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-stephen-ii">Pope Stephen II</a> to seek help from the Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a>. Pepin defeated Aistulf in 754 and 756 and forced him to give the former exarchate to the pope, the basis of the Papal States.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aistulf.jpg" alt="Aistulf" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aistulf_follis_80000860.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aistulf was the son of Duke Pemmo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> and his wife Ratperga. He succeeded his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratchis">Ratchis</a> as duke of Friuli in 744, and became king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> when Ratchis was forced to abdicate and entered a monastery. He was also duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> from 751. Ratchis had been more tolerant of the Roman population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, whereas Aistulf followed a more aggressive policy of expansion and raids against the Papal States and the Byzantine exarchate of Ravenna. The Liber Pontificalis, a hostile papal source, calls him shameless and given to pernicious savagery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Aistulf captured Ravenna and the provinces subject to the exarchate, declared himself king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and held court in the exarch's former palace. He demanded tribute from the Roman duchy itself, which strained the papal treasury and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-stephen-ii">Pope Stephen II</a> to open negotiations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. In 753 Stephen crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and wintered with them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a>'s brother Carloman came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> under pressure from Aistulf to dissuade Pepin from entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, but Pepin left for Italy with the pope in the summer of 754, and Stephen gave Pepin and his son the title Patrician of the Romans. Pepin's army put the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> to flight and besieged Pavia, and a treaty returned the occupied lands.</p><p>Aistulf broke it once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> had left and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 756, which brought a further appeal from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. Pepin returned, defeated him again, and this time had the Franks supervise the return of land. He refused a Byzantine envoy's claim to the provinces and imposed an annual tribute on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, which Paolo Delogu calls political subjection. Aistulf also issued nine legal provisions in 750, the last additions to the Lombard law begun by Rothari, which set each man's military duty by the land he held and tightened border control. He died in December 756 of injuries received while hunting and was buried in the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-marino">San Marino</a> in Pavia, which he had founded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>No <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> narrative of his reign survives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-deacon">Paul the Deacon</a>'s Historia Langobardorum ends with the death of Liutprand in 744, so what is known of Aistulf as king comes from hostile papal texts and from his own laws, which historians treat with caution as evidence. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratchis">Ratchis</a> left his monastery to reclaim the throne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a> outmanoeuvred him and became king in March 757. The Lombard kingdom stayed largely intact, but the alliance between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> shaped the later history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aistulf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When King <b>Aistulf</b> seized Ravenna in 751 and threatened Rome, Pope Stephen II crossed the Alps and in 754 met the Frankish king Pepin the Short, whom he anointed at Saint-Denis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Lombard pressure grew in the 8th century; King <b>Aistulf</b> captured Ravenna in 751, killing or expelling the last exarch, Eutychius.</blockquote><a href="/state/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Aistulf</b> took Ravenna in 751 and threatened Rome, the popes turned to the Franks.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Kingdom of the Lombards</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cividale-del-friuli">Cividale del Friuli</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Pavia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.09,13.44&amp;year=746" aria-label="Cividale del Friuli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4867 541 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aistulf"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5317 811h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5317 811h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.09,13.44&amp;year=746">What was here in 746 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
